%% Kubernetes auto-scaling: a pod is added under load, the others discover it and its aircraft move to it
%% (as measured on 2026-10-16, see architecture.md "Kubernetes and auto-scaling")
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant HPA as Auto-scaler (HPA)
    participant K8s as Kubernetes
    participant DNS as DNS (flightops-peers)
    participant P0 as flightops-0 (old holder)
    participant P3 as flightops-3 (new pod)

    Note over HPA: CPU above 70 % of the request
    HPA->>K8s: replicas 3 → 4
    K8s->>P3: create pod: its own PostgreSQL + disk, then the JVM
    K8s->>DNS: publish flightops-3.flightops-peers (not ready yet)
    P0->>DNS: lookup every 5 s
    DNS-->>P0: flightops-3 exists
    Note over P0: "Discovery: [flightops-3] joined"<br/>rendezvous hashing (4 instances, 2 copies): flightops-3 becomes owner of ~1/4<br/>of the aircraft and backup of another ~1/4 - nothing is copied between the old ones<br/>until it answers, it is treated like an instance that is down
    P3->>P0: catch-up: GET /internal/replicas?for=flightops-3
    Note over P0: counts flightops-3 in even if not discovered yet
    P0-->>P3: the flights flightops-3 should hold
    Note over P3: applies them, then reports ready (~40 s after start)
    K8s->>P3: readiness probe OK: load balancer sends it requests
    Note over P0: 10 s after the change and every 60 s: hand-off
    P0->>P3: POST /internal/replicas (each flight P0 no longer holds)
    P3-->>P0: 204 (newer revision wins, a copy it already has changes nothing)
    Note over P0: deletes its own copy once every new holder has it<br/>("Rebalancing: n flight(s) handed off")
